<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- ===========================================================================
  WO-1125 - the advertising-ID permission the rewarded-ad SDK needs.

  WHY A .androidlib: this folder is an Android Library Project; its manifest
  MERGES additively into the final app manifest at gradle build time. Hand-
  authoring a custom main AndroidManifest.xml would REPLACE Unity 6's generated
  activity/launcher manifest (entry-point dependent - Activity vs GameActivity)
  and is the risky path. Same reasoning, same shape, as the sibling
  MobileWalletAdapter.androidlib - see its header.

  NOTE (AGP 8 / Unity 6): no `package=` attribute here - AGP 8 rejects it. The
  module namespace lives in the sibling build.gradle instead.

  ⛔ THIS IS THE ONLY PERMISSION THIS MODULE MAY EVER DECLARE. It exists for one
  reason and the publisher already flagged this app once for "excessive
  permissions for a tower defense game". Anything else needs its own module and
  its own justification.
============================================================================ -->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- ⛔ REQUIRED AT API 33+, WHICH IS US. PlayerSettings pins
         targetSdkVersion = 36 (Assets/Editor/AndroidBuild.cs, WO-1124), and from
         Android 13 an app cannot read the Google Advertising ID without
         declaring this. Google Play Services returns zeros instead.

         WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS: a missing AD_ID does not throw and
         does not warn. The SDK simply gets no advertising identifier, ads serve
         as non-personalised or not at all, and the symptom on our side reads as
         "no fill" - i.e. it looks like ordinary market conditions rather than a
         manifest we forgot. That is a miserable thing to debug from a fill-rate
         graph, which is why it goes in BEFORE the SDK rather than after the
         first disappointing test.

         It is also how the LevelPlay Integration Helper reports a real GAID at
         the bottom of its log: no permission, no GAID line.

         The device-side control stays with the player - Settings > Google > Ads
         offers Reset and Delete, and a deleted advertising ID means apps read
         zeros no matter what this manifest says. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="com.google.android.gms.permission.AD_ID" />

</manifest>
